# Test: an egress modification that cannot fit suppresses the route
# VALIDATES: T1-1 -- buildModifiedPayload reports a failure distinct from "nothing
#   to modify", and the caller suppresses the route instead of forwarding it
#   UNMODIFIED (the leak: an export policy's tag silently not applied).
# PREVENTS: a policy modification that pushes the rebuilt UPDATE past the RFC 8654
#   message ceiling being dropped on the floor while the untagged route still
#   reaches the peer.
#
# Byte arithmetic. The source body is placed EXACTLY on the ceiling, so the
# assertion does not depend on guessing how many bytes the egress policy adds.
#   ze-peer builds one UPDATE from option=update:value=send-bulk with
#   max-msg=65535, the largest message RFC 8654 permits:
#     header                        = 19
#     withdrawn-len(2) + attr-len(2) =  4
#     ORIGIN(4) + AS_PATH(9) + NEXT_HOP(7) = 20
#     NLRI 16373 x /24, 4 bytes each = 65492
#     -> message 65535, body 65516 = maxUpdateBody (forward_build.go)
#   The dest peer's egress policy then adds a LARGE_COMMUNITY: a 4-byte extended
#   header plus 12 bytes per value. needSize therefore EXCEEDS 65516 by
#   construction, whatever else the egress rail contributes, and
#   buildModifiedPayload returns modifyFailureOverflow.
#
# Why the fixture is generated rather than a hex literal: a 65535-byte message is
# 131070 hex characters. That is past the 64 KiB line limit of both .ci scanners
# (internal/test/tmpfs/tmpfs.go, internal/test/peer/expect.go) and past what any
# reviewer can check. send-bulk states the same bytes in one line.
#
# Why not the previous fixture: it grew a LARGE_COMMUNITY with 300 configured
# values against a 1047-byte body, and relied on the old build's len(payload)+256
# slack buffer overflowing. Commit a1aec5e6c replaced that with an exactly-sized
# one-pass rebuild, so two things changed. The output buffer can no longer be too
# small, and 300 values now exceed AttrPlan.Op's 256-operation cap (a fragment
# stores its operation index in a uint8), which refuses during PLANNING and
# reports reason=handler-fault. 10 values stay well inside that cap, so the
# refusal under test is the size ceiling and not the operation count.

# Source peer (ASN 65001) on 127.0.0.1
stdin=source:terminator=EOF_SOURCE
option=tcp_connections:value=1
option=timeout:value=30s
# The source must outlive the DEST peer's assertion. Without linger the source
# closes as soon as its own EOR matches, ze reads that as session-down and
# withdraws its routes, and the dest's absence assertion then passes because
# there was nothing left to forward -- the wrong reason. Same rationale as
# community-strip.ci and role-otc-egress-filter.ci.
option=linger:value=true
# One UPDATE at the RFC 8654 ceiling. eor is omitted: ze's own initial-sync EOR
# is what the expect below matches, and a second EOR from the peer would not
# change the forwarding decision under test.
option=update:value=send-bulk:prefix=10.0.0.0/24:count=16373:next-hop=10.0.0.1:origin-as=65001:max-msg=65535
expect=bgp:conn=1:seq=1:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF00170200000000
EOF_SOURCE

# Dest peer (ASN 65002) on 127.0.0.2. The egress community tag cannot be applied
# to this route, so this peer must see its own End-of-RIB and nothing else.
stdin=dest:terminator=EOF_DEST
option=tcp_connections:value=1
option=timeout:value=30s
# The dest must still BE an established forward target when the source's route
# arrives, or selectForwardTargets skips it and the route is withheld because
# there is no destination rather than because the modification failed.
option=linger:value=true
expect=bgp:conn=1:seq=1:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF00170200000000
EOF_DEST

tmpfs=test-oversize.run:mode=755:terminator=EOF_RUN
#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Assert ze learned the route and withheld it from the peer whose tag would not fit.

The dest peer's expect=bgp rule asserts an ABSENCE, and an absence is satisfied
just as well by a daemon that never got as far as deciding. So both halves are
required: the source's UPDATE was received (ze had something to leak), and the
dest was sent nothing beyond its own End-of-RIB (ze did not leak it).
"""
import sys
from ze_api import API, runtime_fail


def main():
    api = API()
    api.declare_done()
    api.wait_for_config()
    api.capability_done()
    api.wait_for_registry()
    api.ready()

    api.assert_peer_received_only_eor('127.0.0.2', source_addr='127.0.0.1',
                                      expected_peers=2)

    print('OK: route accepted from the source, nothing beyond EOR sent to the dest',
          file=sys.stderr)
    api.dispatch('request shutdown')
    api.wait_for_shutdown()


if __name__ == '__main__':
    main()
EOF_RUN

# ze configuration: the source peer negotiates RFC 8654 so ze accepts a 65535-byte
# UPDATE; the dest peer tags large communities on egress, which cannot be written
# without pushing the rebuilt body past the message ceiling.
stdin=ze-bgp:terminator=EOF_CONF
plugin {
	internal adj-rib-in {
		use bgp-adj-rib-in
	}
	internal rs {
		use bgp-rs
	}
	external test-oversize {
		run ./test-oversize.run
		encoder json
	}
}
bgp {
	community {
		large oversize {
			value [ 65000:1:0 65000:1:1 65000:1:2 65000:1:3 65000:1:4 65000:1:5 65000:1:6 65000:1:7 65000:1:8 65000:1:9 ]
		}
	}

	peer source-peer {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 127.0.0.1
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.1
				accept false
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 65000
				remote 65001
			}
			router-id 1.2.3.4
			family {
				ipv4/unicast { prefix { maximum 100000; } }
			}
			capability {
				extended-message enable
			}
		}
		attach process rs {
			receive [ update-received state open-received refresh ]
			send [ update ]
		}
		attach process adj-rib-in {
			receive [ update-received state ]
			send [ update ]
		}
	}

	peer dest-peer {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 127.0.0.2
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.2
				accept false
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 65000
				remote 65002
			}
			router-id 1.2.3.4
			family {
				ipv4/unicast { prefix { maximum 100000; } }
			}
		}

		filter {
			egress {
				community {
					tag oversize
				}
			}
		}
		attach process rs {
			receive [ update-received state open-received refresh ]
			send [ update ]
		}
		attach process adj-rib-in {
			receive [ update-received state ]
			send [ update ]
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

# Process orchestration
cmd=background:seq=1:exec=ze-peer --port $PORT:stdin=source
cmd=background:seq=2:exec=ze-peer --bind 127.0.0.2 --port $PORT:stdin=dest
cmd=foreground:seq=3:exec=ze --plugin ze.bgp-filter-community -:stdin=ze-bgp:timeout=30s
expect=exit:code=0
# The suppression, with the reason. Matched as a PATTERN rather than the
# forward rail's exact phrase because two rails can deliver this route and each
# names itself: reactor_api_forward.go says "egress modification failed,
# suppressing route" and reactor_api_batch.go says "stale re-advertise
# modification failed, suppressing route". Which one runs depends on whether the
# dest was already an established forward target, which is a scheduling
# property, not a property of the behaviour under test. Both share this phrase.
# reason=overflow pins WHICH modifyFailure, so a future change that suppresses
# for a different reason does not quietly inherit this test's green. In
# particular it separates the size ceiling from handler-fault, which is what the
# 256-operation cap reports.
#
# These two lines do NOT prove the suppression, and must not be read as if they
# did. recordModifyFailureAddr runs BEFORE the `continue` that withholds the
# route, so both still match when the guard is removed: a mutation run that
# deleted the guard leaked 17 UPDATEs to 127.0.0.2 and these two expectations
# stayed green. What caught it was the observer's absence assertion. Keep both:
# the log pins the REASON, the observer pins the EFFECT.
expect=stderr:pattern=modification failed, suppressing route
expect=stderr:contains=peer=127.0.0.2 reason=overflow
reject=stderr:pattern=panic recovered
reject=stderr:pattern=fatal error
reject=stderr:pattern=ZE-OBSERVER-FAIL
